this morning on the dish in an  unassuming new york city eatery with a  mighty profile brooklyn's meadow suite  is in the literal shadow of the  williamsburg bridge and for nearly its  entire existence it's had the attention  of the food world michael george made a  visit and he's here to share a taste  with us good morning michael michelle  good morning meadowsweet is the creation  of michelin star chef polo dobkin and  his wife stephanie lemper for the last  eight years they've been giving diners a  taste of the countryside in the big city  chef polo dobkin trained with some of  new york's top chefs but now at his  brooklyn restaurant meadow suite he's  found his own voice  and he expresses that voice by letting  his ingredients do the talking  seasonality simplicity and really  letting flavors kind of speak for  themselves  trying not to have anything be  overwrought or overworked sourcing some  of his best ingredients from his dad's  own garden in the hudson valley he has a  very green thumb when we first opened we  just started basically working together  planting and bringing down going up  every weekend and bringing down produce  and we basically create our menus on a  weekly basis  his creations modern american cuisine  with mediterranean inspiration like  these crispy baby artichokes topped with  shaved lemon parmesan and a creamy  garlic dressing oh this is great it  tastes very fresh  the born and raised new yorker was  already helping in the kitchen at the  age of five who did the cooking growing  up  my father did most of the cooking and  then when i got to a certain age i just  kind of started helping out did you have  the bug from the beginning or did it  take time to grow  well you know what looking back on it i  think i had the bug from the beginning  and that bug led him to volunteer at a  culinary school while paying the bills  as a bike messenger i just started  volunteering you know one day a week  then one day a week left to two days a  week and then two days a week led to  three days a week and when i got to that  point i told myself i should probably  just  get a job in restaurant and see how it  goes  he studied at the french culinary  institute and worked at new york's  gramercy tavern and dressler dress was a  wonderful restaurant one of the first to  get a michelin star in brooklyn sort of  ahead of its time if you want to say  back then it was just  us peter luger's and diner in this neck  of the woods  his wife stephanie who now helps run  meadowsweet came up with the idea of  starting their own place she pushed  really hard to go out on her own which  takes a lot of courage took a while to  persuade me to do it why did it take  some arm twisting i it's just you know  leaving your comfort zone and doing  something as risky as opening a  restaurant can take a lot  they chose to set up in williamsburg in  the exact spot where dobkin earned his  first michelin star at dressler you  could have picked any location to start  your own place yeah you chose here the  energy  both from the people who live here and  the people that decide to work here the  creative  energy is  is through the roof  at meadow suite customers get familiar  dishes with some surprises  like this burrata with figs pistachio  basil pesto and pickled kumquats and  tirine pasta with chanterelle mushrooms  and shaved summer truffle that is  fantastic  it's so creamy it's rich well this one's  all about we're going for decadence  by putting an emphasis on seasonality  meadowsuite earned a michelin star just  months after opening what did that mean  to you i was it was meant the world it  was hoping that inspectors would come  and was hoping to get a mission to start  did not expect to get it that quickly  and we've been blessed to be able to  keep it  for as long as we have his dishes with  their vibrant colors and flavors evoke  images of summer like scottish salmon on  a bed of black rice with coconut curry  oh this is different  this is not what i was expecting we make  a fresh green curry in house  and just infuse a little bit of coconut  or infuse a little coconut milk with it  and that's the foundation for the dish  like the food the indoor herb garden  might make you forget for a moment  you're in the middle of new york city it  feels like a little taste of the country  here in the city well that was  definitely part of the plan of the  design using fresh simple dishes who's  not going to love that  that's amazing to take diners on an  escape to the countryside tell me about  the customers here what do you want them  to feel when they come into meadow suite  we want them to feel comfortable  relaxed happy um and looking forward to  a wonderful meal  and they bring that same philosophy to  their drinks and so i brought you guys  one of their signature cocktails this is  called a mighty quinn now it's got gin  lemon honey and currants from the hudson  valley cheers guys what's up nice job  where is boom um very nice and i was  gonna explain why they call this a  mighty quinn but i have a feeling you're  about to find out once you take a sip oh  yeah  it's stronger than it that it looks it's  got a little power it's not as strong as  i expected  it's the currents that help balance that  out everything that i had there had this  amazing balance of  fresh flavors and this really this  tastes like summer in a glass it really  does i love that he also uses herbs from  his father's own garden it really  showcases how it's a family business in  some ways there's a whole story in every  single dish that we had there including  the drinks and his father was a big part  of his cooking journey  i love the name meadows sweetie it's  sweet sounds cool right great thank you  michael nice job thanks michael
 

